## Definitions added in this part

The vocabulary of part 1 carries over: a print is one venue's fills within one second on one outcome and one taker side; a pair is fresh when both venues' latest prints are at most F seconds old; an observation is a print on either leg while the other leg is fresh. Two additions:

| Term | Meaning here |
| --- | --- |
| Route | A direction (which venue holds YES: `PM YES + K NO` or `K YES + PM NO`) and a role for each leg: a **taker** crosses, a **maker** rests. Route names give the Polymarket role first, then the Kalshi role. A taker leg is priced from the print on the side it would hit; a resting leg from the print on the side that would fill it, so a taker-sell print is the reference for a resting bid. |
| Reference net edge | `1 − Polymarket cost − Kalshi cost − fees` per contract pair, both legs priced from their latest prints at an observation. Positive means the two references overlapped after fees. It does not mean either leg would have filled. |

Each route reads one of part 1's taker-action pairs, one print per venue:

| Route (PM role / Kalshi role) | `PM YES + K NO` reads | `K YES + PM NO` reads |
| --- | --- | --- |
| taker/taker | PM buy / K sell | PM sell / K buy |
| taker/maker | PM buy / K buy | PM sell / K sell |
| maker/taker | PM sell / K sell | PM buy / K buy |
| maker/maker | PM sell / K buy | PM buy / K sell |

The gross edge before fees is that pair's signed gap from part 1, read in the route's favour: Kalshi minus Polymarket for `PM YES + K NO`, Polymarket minus Kalshi for `K YES + PM NO`. The maker/maker pairs are the cross-side ones, so what that route reads is the cross-venue bid–ask spread.

### Fees

Neither venue charges a flat fee. Both charge `rate × p × (1 − p)` per contract, largest at 50 c and zero at the extremes, where p is the price of the leg being bought (a NO leg pays on 1 − p; the curve is symmetric). Rates are the venues' published schedules, not historical billing, and no rebates are assumed:

{{ rates }}

Two taker fees at 50 c cost {{ k.fee_round_trip }} c per contract pair, against a mean absolute gap of {{ k.gap.pl.abs }}–{{ k.gap.ucl.abs }} c in the club groups (part 1). A maker/maker pair pays {{ k.fee_mm }} c.

## 1. What survives fees

At every route-leg print with the other leg fresh, both legs are priced from their latest prints and the fee model is subtracted, at each group's cutoff from part 1 (60 s for the club groups, 5 s for the World Cup in play, 10 s for its last pregame hour).

{{ chart('21:0') }}

Bars show the share of fresh observations whose reference net edge is above zero. **The remainder, 100% minus the bar, is at or below zero.** For the three routes with a taker leg, that is the large majority in every group, outcome and direction: taker/taker is positive on {{ k.screen.tt_pl_all_1 }} of Premier League in-play observations for `PM YES + K NO` and {{ k.screen.tt_pl_all_2 }} for `K YES + PM NO`, with a mean net result of {{ k.screen.tt_pl_all_1_net }} c; in the World Cup in play {{ k.screen.tt_wc_all_1 }} and {{ k.screen.tt_wc_all_1_net }} c; in its last pregame hour {{ k.screen.tt_wcpre_all_1 }}. The one-taker routes are positive on {{ k.route_range.taker_maker.lo }}–{{ k.route_range.taker_maker.hi }} (taker/maker) and {{ k.route_range.maker_taker.lo }}–{{ k.route_range.maker_taker.hi }} (maker/taker) of observations, and their mean net edge is negative in every cell ({{ k.route_range.taker_maker.net_lo }} to {{ k.route_range.taker_maker.net_hi }} c and {{ k.route_range.maker_taker.net_lo }} to {{ k.route_range.maker_taker.net_hi }} c). After the fee curve, any route that crosses a book loses money on most last-trade observations.

Maker/maker is the only route positive on more than half of its observations: {{ k.screen.mm_pl_all_1 }} (`PM YES + K NO`) and {{ k.screen.mm_pl_all_2 }} (`K YES + PM NO`) in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_all_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_all_2 }} in the Champions League, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_all_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_wc_all_2 }} in the World Cup in play, {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_all_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_all_2 }} in its last pregame hour, with a mean net edge of {{ k.screen.mm_pl_all_1_net }} c and {{ k.screen.mm_pl_all_2_net }} c in the Premier League and {{ k.screen.mm_wc_all_1_net }} c and {{ k.screen.mm_wc_all_2_net }} c in the World Cup in play. What this route reads is the cross-venue bid–ask spread of part 1 section 4: a bid-side print on one venue against an ask-side print on the other, which sat 1–1.5 c apart in the club groups. Seen after the fact, those two resting-side references overlap after fees on most observations. It has the shape of a cross-venue market-making position, not an arbitrage: both resting orders have to fill, and the tape does not say how often they would.

Within maker/maker, the direction follows the outcome. For team wins, `PM YES + K NO` is ahead in every group: {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_2 }} in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_team_2 }} in the Champions League, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wc_team_2 }} in the World Cup in play and {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_team_2 }} in its last hour. For draws, `K YES + PM NO` carries the larger mean net edge in every group ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_2_net }} against {{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_1_net }} c in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_2_net }} against {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_1_net }} c in the Champions League, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_2_net }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_1_net }} c in the World Cup in play) and the higher positive share in three of them ({{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_2 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_1 }}, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_2 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_1 }}, {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_draw_2 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_draw_1 }}); the Premier League is level ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_2 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_1 }}). Part 1 section 4 is the reason: for team wins the wider cross-venue spread is Polymarket's bid side against Kalshi's ask side, which `PM YES + K NO` reads; on draws the wider one is the other.

