## 2. Across the freshness cutoff

The same screen at every cutoff from 1 to {{ sweep_max }} s (the export runs to 300 s; the curves are flat long before). The dotted line marks each group's F.

{{ chart('22:0') }}

The curves are almost flat. In the Premier League, maker/maker is positive on {{ k.sweep.maker_maker_pl_1 }} of observations at F = 1 s, {{ k.sweep.maker_maker_pl_10 }} at 10 s, {{ k.sweep.maker_maker_pl_60 }} at 60 s, {{ k.sweep.maker_maker_pl_120 }} at 120 s and {{ k.sweep.maker_maker_pl_300 }} at 300 s; taker/taker moves from {{ k.sweep.taker_taker_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.taker_taker_pl_300 }}, taker/maker from {{ k.sweep.taker_maker_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.taker_maker_pl_300 }}, and maker/taker from {{ k.sweep.maker_taker_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.maker_taker_pl_300 }}. This is the fee-adjusted version of part 1 section 3.3: the cutoff decides how much of the clock is compared, not what the comparison finds.

The slow rise of maker/maker with F, about three points from 1 s to 300 s, has one plausible reading that is not tested here: a looser cutoff admits quieter stretches, in which each venue's own resting-side prices sit further apart, so two resting references overlap more often. Whatever its cause, a looser cutoff admits staler references without visibly changing the rate, which is a reason to keep F tight when the result is used.

## 3. Resting on both venues: direction and freshness

Because F is swept here, each column is a competition × window and the selected cutoff plays no role. Each direction of maker/maker reads one taker-action pair: `PM YES + K NO` prices Polymarket's resting YES bid from the Polymarket sell print and Kalshi's resting NO bid from the Kalshi buy print (`PM sell / K buy`); `K YES + PM NO` reads `PM buy / K sell`; pooled sums both directions and uses the venue-level pair for the clock states.

{{ chart('24:0') }}

The six rows share the freshness axis:

- **Clock states** (rows 1–3): the four states of part 1 section 2.2 for the pair each direction reads, at F = 5, 10, …, 60 s. In the Premier League, `PM YES + K NO` has both legs fresh on {{ k.mm.pl_both_1_60 }} of the in-play clock at 60 s and `K YES + PM NO` on {{ k.mm.pl_both_2_60 }}; at 10 s the first direction is at {{ k.mm.pl_both_1_10 }}.
- **Positive rate** (row 4): `Positive rate = P(maker/maker net reference edge > 0 | fresh print observation)`. It is flat in F, and the direction order is stable within each group: `PM YES + K NO` above `K YES + PM NO` in the club groups and in the World Cup in play, and by a wide margin in the World Cup's last hour ({{ k.mm.wcpre_pos_1_10 }} against {{ k.mm.wcpre_pos_2_10 }} at 10 s).
- **Positive standing** (row 5): `Positive standing = positive fresh seconds / total clock seconds`, the product of "both legs fresh" and "positive given fresh". It rises with F because the first factor does: in the Premier League, `PM YES + K NO` stands positive for {{ k.mm.pl_open_1_10 }} of the clock at 10 s and {{ k.mm.pl_open_1_60 }} at 60 s; in the World Cup in play, {{ k.mm.wc_open_1_5 }} already at 5 s and {{ k.mm.wc_open_1_60 }} at 60 s.
- **Mean net edge** (row 6): cents after fees per fresh print (solid) and per fresh second (dashed). Club groups sit around 1 c and the World Cup around 0.6 c in play; the per-second line is slightly higher, meaning positive references last a little longer than negative ones.

A positive edge here says that the two venues' resting-side prints overlapped after fees. It says nothing about whether either resting order would have filled, and a resting order that fills is the one on the wrong side of the next move more often than not. That adverse selection is unmeasured here.

