## 5. How often, and for how long

The frequency of maker/maker positives per group at its F: positive observations per fixture-hour (fixtures × window length as the denominator) and the time-weighted share of the clock during which a fresh, positive reference is standing. The whole-clock share uses six clocks per fixture (three outcomes × two directions); the fresh-clock share counts only seconds with a fresh pair.

{{ frequency }}

Positives are frequent and spread out rather than concentrated: {{ k.freq.all_have }} fixture in every group shows at least one, and the ten busiest fixtures account for {{ k.freq.top10_min }}–{{ k.freq.top10_max }} of the total. A positive resting-side overlap stands for {{ k.freq.pl_clock }} of the Premier League in-play clock, {{ k.freq.ucl_clock }} in the Champions League, {{ k.freq.wc_clock }} in the World Cup in play and {{ k.freq.wcpre_clock }} in its last pregame hour, and for {{ k.freq.pl_fresh }}–{{ k.freq.ucl_fresh }} of the seconds in which a fresh pair exists. Several observations can belong to one continuing overlap, so these are not counts of independent trades.

## 6. Where to look first

Parts 1 and 2 read together, as a ranking of what the tape supports most clearly. Every line is a hindsight description of reference prices, not a result.

- **Rest on both venues.** Maker/maker is the only route positive on more than half of its observations, in every group, outcome and direction; every route that crosses a book has a negative mean net edge everywhere (section 1).
- **Hold YES on Polymarket for team wins.** `PM YES + K NO` leads `K YES + PM NO` by 10–30 points in every group ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_2 }} in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_team_1 }} against {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_team_2 }} in the World Cup's last hour), because Polymarket's bid side sits furthest below Kalshi's ask side (part 1 section 4).
- **Hold YES on Kalshi for draws.** `K YES + PM NO` carries the larger mean net edge on draws in every group ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_2_net }} against {{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_1_net }} c in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_2_net }} against {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_1_net }} c in the Champions League), because on draws the wider cross-venue spread is the other one.
- **Put the higher-priced leg on Polymarket's YES side.** The mid-price fee penalty is `K YES + PM NO`'s: it falls to {{ k.price.pl_2_mid }} at 30–70 c in the Premier League while `PM YES + K NO` holds {{ k.price.pl_1_mid }} there and {{ k.price.pl_1_high }} at 70–90 c (section 4).
- **It is not rare.** A positive resting-side overlap stands for {{ k.freq.inplay_clock_lo }}–{{ k.freq.inplay_clock_hi }} of the in-play clock, in {{ k.freq.all_have }} fixture, with the ten busiest fixtures holding at most {{ k.freq.top10_max }} of positives (section 5).
- **Club in play against the World Cup in play is a trade-off, not a ranking.** The club groups carry about twice the mean net edge per observation ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_all_1_net }} c against {{ k.screen.mm_wc_all_1_net }} c for `PM YES + K NO`) but need a 60 s cutoff to keep the clock comparable, which is the single-leg exposure of part 1 section 3.3; the World Cup is thinner and near-instant.
- **Not known.** Whether either resting leg fills, what one leg alone costs when the other does not, and where the price goes after the observation. Part 3 measures these on the same observations.

## What is not measured

Everything above is a description of the trade tape. It establishes where the two venues' references overlap after fees, in which direction, at which price, and for how long. It does not establish that any of it could be captured:

- There are no historical order books, so available size and queue position at the reference prices are unknown.
- A maker/maker positive assumes both passive legs fill. The tape does not give the joint fill probability, and the resting leg that fills is exposed to the next move.
- The price after each observation is part 3's subject. Whether a gap persists, closes toward one venue, or reverses is not examined here, nor are markouts on the resting leg.
- Fees are the venues' current published schedules applied to historical trades; no rebates or tier discounts are assumed.
- No confidence intervals or fixture-clustered uncertainty are supplied. Comparisons are descriptive for this sample.

